\section developer_param_overview_sec Overview

The format of PSCF parameter file consists of a set of nested blocks, as 
discussed \ref user_param_page "here". Each block in this format contains 
the data required to initialize an instance of a particular class. 

The system used by PSCF to read a parameter file relies on C++ inheritance.
Every class that reads a block of the parameter file must be directly or 
indirectly derived from the Util::ParamComposite base class.  Each such 
class must override a virtual function that reads the associated parameter 
file block. The implementation of this function defines the format of the
associated parameter file block, and uses other functions inherited from 
ParamComposite to read individual parameters and nested subblocks.

This design encourages data encapsulation. Most parameters that are read 
from the parameter file are stored in private class member variables. 
Data privacy is maintained because each class is responsible for reading 
the initialization data that it requires.

The format of the parameter file is intentionally designed to mimic 
the ownership hierarchy of objects in the underlying source code. This
connection is reinforced by the use of block labels that correspond to 
the names of classes and class labels. This labelling of blocks by 
class names is designed, in part, to make it easy for users and 
developers to find the code that reads a particular block when needed.

The parameter file format used by PSCF is primarily a fixed format 
file, with some flexibility provided by the use of optional and 
polymorphic blocks. Because the system that reads a parameter file 
knows what item or set of possible options to expect at each position 
in the file, it can immediately detect most syntax errors, and can
usually provide a clear error message that explains the nature of 
the problem.  The decision to use a relatively rigid file format 
for the parameter file was made chosen, in part, to simplify design 
and maintenance of code that initializes a PSCF program.  A more 
flexible format is used for the command file, which is read and
processed after the parameter file.

The ParamComposite class contains a data structure that stores a record 
of the format of a parameter file block while it is being read. The format 
is stored as a tree of nodes, in which each node represents an item in a
parameter file. A node associated with a parameter stores the associated 
label and a pointer to the variable that holds the parameter value.  This 
mechanism can be used to print a parameter file block or an entire 
parameter file after it has been read.

\see Util::ParamComposite

\section developer_param_classname_sec Setting the class name label

A parameter file block associated with an instance of a subclass of
ParamComposite class begins with a line containing a label for the class, 
which is generally the name of the class or class template, followed by 
an opening curly bracket. For example, the opening and closing lines of a 
block associated with a class named "Domain" looks something like this
\code
   Domain{
     ...
   }
\endcode
where ellipses represented the omitted body of this block. When the code 
that reads a required block of a parameter file reads the first line, it 
compares the label that precedes the opening curly bracket to an expected 
value, and halts execution if these strings do not exactly match.

The expected value for the class name label that appears in this opening 
line is set by a function named "setClassName" that is inherited from 
the Util::ParamComposite base class. This function has the interface
\code
  void setClassName(char const * className)
\endcode
Here, the className parameter is a string literal that gives the required 
label. The setClassName function is usually called in the constructor of 
a subclass of ParamComposite, and passed a literal string argument that 
is normally the name of the relevant class or class template. 

\section developer_param_readparameters_sec The readParameters function

The format of the body of a parameter file block that is read by a subclass 
of ParamComposite is usually defined by the implementation of an overridden 
virtual function Util::ParamComposite::readParameters.  The interface of 
this function is
\code
   virtual void readParameters(std::istream& in);
\endcode
The ParamComposite class defines an empty (do-nothing) default 
implementation for this function.  Each subclass of ParamComposite may 
override this by defining non-trivial implementation in order to define 
a format for the body of the associated parameter file block. 

The readParameters function is usually called by another virtual protected 
member function of ParamComposite named Util::ParamComposite::readParam. 
Like the readParameters function, the readParam function takes a 
std::iostream input stream as its only parameter. The difference is that 
the readParam function reads the lines containing the opening and closing 
braces of a parameter file block, in addition to the body of the block, 
while readParameters only reads the body of the block that appears indented
between the opening and closing lines. ParamComposite defines a default 
implementation of the readParam function that reads the opening line of 
a parameter file block, checks the block label, calls the virtual 
readParameters function to read the body of the block, and then reads 
the line containing a closing brace.  The readParam method is also 
declared virtual, but most subclasses use the default implementation.

Most subclasses of ParamComposite override the readParameters function 
to define the format for the body of the parameter block, and simply use 
the default implementation of the inherited readParam function to add 
opening and closing lines.  

\section developer_param_parameters_sec Reading individual parameters

The implementation of an overridden readParameters() function normally
uses a set of protected function templates that are inherited from the 
Util::ParamComposite class to read parameters and subblocks. The simplest 
of these are the read and readOptional function templates, either of 
which can be used to read a single parameter of any type.

Util::ParamComposite::read is a function template that takes the type
of the value of a parameter as a template parameter.  The input interface 
for this function template is given by
\code
   template <int T>
   read(std::istream& in, const char* label, T& data)
\endcode
where template parameter T denotes the relevant data type. This function
template is used to read a required parameter.

The ParamComposite::readOptional function template is used to read an
optional parameter but is otherwise identical to the read template.
This function template has an input interface 
\code
   template <int T>
   readOptional(std::istream& in, const char* label, T& data)
\endcode

For both of these function templates:

   - parameter "in" represents an input stream associated with a 
     parameter file.  

   - parameter "label" is a string literal that gives the expected 
     value of the label string for a parameter or a parameter block. 

   - parameter "data" is a reference to the variable that will hold 
     the parameter value extracted from the parameter file.

For simplicity, the type of the value that is returned by either of 
these functions has been omitted from both of the above function
signatures.  The return type has been hidden in the above descriptions
the return value is actually almost never retained or used, and because
the return value is an instance of somewhat a specialized type, 
Util::ScalarParam<T>.  Both of these function templates are thus 
usually used as if they were void functions, with no return value.

The Util::ParamComposite::read function template is used for required 
parameters. If this function reads a line that begins with a string that 
does not exactly match the string passed as the "label" parameter, it 
prints a message that shows both the expected label and the label that 
was actually read, and then throws an Exception to halt program execution.
The readOptional function template is used for optional parametrs, and so 
will exit without throwing an Exception if it reads a label that does not
match the "label" parameter, allowing the program to continue processing
the parameter file block. When the readOptional reads a label that does
not match its "label" parameter, it stores the label was read from file
in a buffer that is accessed by other functions that read parameters or 
subblocks, until one such function either matches that label or throws
an Exception. 

The read and readOptional function templates can be used to read a 
parameter of any primitive C/C++ type such as int or double, or parameters 
that are instances of any class for which there exists overloaded stream 
input (\>\>) and stream output (\<\<) operators. A separate set of 
inherited function templates are used for array-valued parameters, as 
discussed below.

<b>Example</b>: As a simple example, consider the following 
skeleton of the definition for a class named A: 
\code

   using namepace Util;

   class A : public ParamComposite
   {

   public:

      /// Default constructor.
      A();

      /// Read the body of the parameter file block.
      void readParameters(std::istream& in) override;

      // other member functions

   private:

      int    var1_;
      double var2_;
   }
\endcode
Class A has an int member variable var1_, and a double precision
real member variable var2_. Suppose that values of both var1_ and 
var2_ need to be read from a parameter file block, and that the 
desired parameter block format for a block associated with class A 
is as shown below: 
\verbatim
A{
  var1     int
  var2*    double
}
\endverbatim
This parameter file format is shown in a 
\ref user_param_format_page "standard form", in which the strings 
var1 and var2 represent literal parameter label strings, the 
typenames int and double indicate the required types of the 
corresponding parameter values, and the asterisk after the var2 label 
indicates that var2 is an \ref user_param_format_page "optional" 
double precision parameter.  

Minimal implementations of the constructor and readParameters member
functions required to parse this parameter block format are show below:
\code

   A::A()
   { setClassName("A"); }

   void A::readParameters(std::istream& in)
   {
      var2_ = 0.0;
      read(in, "var1", var1_);
      readOptional(in, "var2", var2_);
   }

\endcode
The label string "A" that appears in the opening line of the block 
is passed an argument to the setClassName class in the constructor.
The required label strings "var1", and "var2" for individual
parameters are instead passed as parameters to the read and 
readOptional functions.  The line "var2_ = 0.0" assigns a zero default 
value to parameter var2_, which will be used if a line containing this 
optional parameter does not appear in the parameter file.

Often, the implementation of a readParameters() method also contains 
code to validate input values, allocate any required memory, and take
any other actions that are required to initialize an instance of the 
class but require knowledge of values of parameters that are read from
the parameter file.  No such additional initialization steps are needed 
for this simple example.

\section developer_param_array_sec Reading array valued parameters

The ParaComposite class also provides several other protected member
functions that can be used by subclass to read array- and matrix-valued 
parameters, for which values of multiple elements can be placed in any 
of several different types of container.  

For example, the readDArray and readOptionalDArray function
templates  are designed to read required and optional array-valued 
parameters for which the elements are stored DArray containers.  Like 
the read and readOptional functions, these are function templates for 
which type of each element is a template parameter.  The interface for 
the ParamComposite::readDArray function template (without the type of 
the seldom-used return value) is:
\code
template <typename T>
readDArray(std::istream &in, const char *label, DArray<Type>& array, int n)
\endcode
The readDArrayOptional member function template has an identical 
interface. For both of these function templates, the "in" and "label" 
parameters have the same meaning as for individual parameters, while:

   - parameter "array" is a reference to a DArray whose elements 
     will hold an array parameter values

   - n is the expected number of elements in the array

The parameter file format for an array-valued parameter starts with a line
containing the label string following by an opening square bracket (\[) 
followed by elements values on separate lines and ending with a closing 
square bracket (\]), as discussed \ref user_param_format_page previously .

The ParamComposite base class provides several other protected member 
functions that subclasses can use read arrays and matrices of parameters 
into other types of containers. These include functions designe to store
values in a bare C arrays, a fixed size FArray containers, and a DMatrix 
matrix container. 

Please consult the developer documentation for the Util::ParamComposite 
class for more complete information.

\section developer_param_subblocks_sec Reading nested subblocks

The ParamComposite class also provides two protected inherited
functions named readParamComposite and readOptionalParamComposite
that may be used by subclasses to read nested subblocks.
Slightly simplified versions of interfaces for these functions 
(omitting an optional parameter that is rarely used) are
\code
void readParamComposite(std::istream in, ParamComposite &child)
\endcode
and 
\code
void readParamCompositeOptional(std::istream in, ParamComposite &child)
\endcode
Here, "parameter" in is an input parameter file stream, while 
"child" is a reference to an instance of a class that is another 
subclass of ParamComposite which must read a nested subblock. Both
functions are designed to read a nested subblock associated with
a member of another subclass that of ParamComposite.  The function 
readParamComposite is used for required subblocks, while 
readParamCompositeOptional is used for optional subblocks. 

<b> Example </b>:
To see how this works, we consider  slightly more complicated
version of the class named A that is defined above. We suppose for 
this purpose that A has a third member variable that holds an 
instance of another class named B, as shown below:
below:
\code

   using namepace Util;

   class A : public ParamComposite
   {

   public:

      // Default constructor
      A();

      // Read the body of the parameter file
      void readParameters(std::istream& in);

      // other member functions

   private:

      int    var1_;
      double var2_;
      B      varB_;
   }
\endcode
We also suppose that B is itself a subclass of ParamComposite, 
and that the parameter file format for class A includes a nested
subblock that is associated with the variable varB_ of type B, 
giving the following parameter file format:
\verbatim
A{
  var1    int
  var2*   double
  B{ ... }
}
\endverbatim
Here, ellipses (...) are used to indicate the omitted indented body 
of a nested parameter file subblock associated with variable varB_.
In this example, var1 and the B block are required, while var2 is
optional.  

A minimal version of the readParameters function required to read
this modified format is shown below
\code

   void A::readParameters(std::istream& in)
   {
      read(in, "var1", var1_);
      readOptional(in, "var2", var2_);
      readParamComposite(in, varB_);
   }

\endcode
The readParamCompositeOptional function would have been used to
read the nest block associatd with varB_ if that block was 
optional rather than required. 

Note that the readParamComposite or readParamCompositeOptional 
functions do not require a label string as an input parameter. This 
is because each subclass of ParamComposite must instead use the 
inherited "setClassName" function to define the class name label 
that should appear in the opening line of an associated parameter 
file block. In this example, all constructors of class B are 
thus assumed to contain the statement setClassName("B").

